&lt;p&gt;Presentation by&amp;nbsp;Bill Holt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lithosphere and mantle dynamics in the Basin and Range Province and Colorado Plateau: landscape evolution and Grand Canyon development&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The Basin and Range Province and the Colorado Plateau provide an exceptional setting to study how deep Earth processes shape landscapes. The relative roles of crustal rebound, mantle-driven uplift, and lithospheric forces in driving Cenozoic extension and canyon formation remain debated. We use numerical models that combine mantle flow, lithospheric stresses, plate boundary changes, and surface processes to reconstruct the region&amp;rsquo;s history since the late Eocene. Our results show that high topography supported by a thick crustal root generated strong gravitational forces that drove large-scale crustal stretching and the rise of metamorphic core complexes as the crust collapsed. These forces, together with the shift from subduction to Pacific&amp;ndash;North America plate motion, explain the observed directions and magnitudes of extension. Slab rollback and related mantle flow mainly weakened the lithosphere by adding heat, melts, and fluids. The models also reproduce drainage reorganizations&amp;mdash;from northeast-directed flow onto the Colorado Plateau, to later southward and then southwestward flow&amp;mdash;helping explain the timing and pathways of Grand Canyon development. Together, these results highlight how mantle processes, lithosphere dynamics, and surface evolution combine to sculpt landscapes in southwestern North America.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI in Bioinformatics for Forensic Investigative Genetic Genealogy DNA sequencing&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dr. Daniel S. Hellwig&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Dr. Daniel S. Hellwig brings over 20 years of expertise in forensic DNA science to his role as Chief Executive Officer, where he leads with a focus on innovation and operational excellence. A recognized thought leader in forensic investigative genetic genealogy (FIGG), Dr. Hellwig spearheaded the accreditation of the first laboratory globally for whole genome sequencing of forensic SNPs and the second for the Verogen Kintelligence kit, solidifying his reputation for advancing the capabilities of forensic DNA analysis.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Throughout his career, Dr. Hellwig has directed the development and implementation of cutting-edge sequencing technologies, ensuring laboratories remain at the forefront of forensic science. His extensive experience spans strategic laboratory management, forensic casework, and collaboration with law enforcement and genealogical communities. Under his leadership, the laboratory continues to set new standards in solving complex cases and advancing the field of FIGG.&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The global translation and knowledge turns in the history of science have highlighted the contingency of the Anglophone term &amp;lsquo;science&amp;rsquo; in several non-Western contexts. Several scholars have attended to the historical transformation of knowledge into science across languages, imperial geographies, and epistemological traditions. This talk joins these efforts by following science as an epistemic concept and as an actor&amp;rsquo;s category in the lay, vernacular setting of a popular-science periodical in colonial South Asia. At the turn of the twentieth century, when some Indian intellectuals presented modern scientific knowledge to lay readers, they invoked science not as &lt;em&gt;śāstra&lt;/em&gt;&amp;mdash;a millennia-old concept and term for scientific discourse among Sanskrit learned communities&amp;mdash;but as &lt;em&gt;vij&amp;ntilde;āna&lt;/em&gt;. What was at stake in this turn to a new, or rather, a renewed old epistemic concept, and what was the scope and meaning of science thus re-described? This talk will trace the vernacular meanings these categories acquired (and lost) as Hindi authors rendered modern scientific knowledge into public knowledge in the pages of &lt;em&gt;Vigyan&lt;/em&gt;, a science monthly inaugurated in 1915. A range of science enthusiasts&amp;mdash;Hindi literati, scholars of Sanskrit natural philosophy and Ayurveda, and science graduates and professors at provincial universities&amp;mdash;published reflections on science as &lt;em&gt;vij&amp;ntilde;āna &lt;/em&gt;in &lt;em&gt;Vigyan&lt;/em&gt;. Charu Singh will show the multiple discursive strategies at work for translating knowledge and constituting the &amp;quot;Hindi reader with a taste for science&amp;quot; in early-twentieth century north India.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Event Speakers&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hps.cam.ac.uk/directory/singh" target="_blank"&gt;Charu Singh&lt;/a&gt;, University Assistant Professor in Non-Western History of the Sciences at the University of Cambridge&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Discussant: &lt;a href="https://www.qc.cuny.edu/academics/history/full-time-faculty/" target="_blank"&gt;Andrew Amstutz&lt;/a&gt;, Assistant Professor of History at Queens College&lt;/li&gt;
